      <article class="note">
        <span class="draft">Draft</span>
        <h2>Dry rocks lie</h2>
        <p>Most beach stones look like the same tan gravel when they are dry. Wet them and the real color, banding, and pattern show up. That is why you see people at the waterline dunking, spraying, and yes, licking rocks.</p>
        <p class="src">Source: <a href="https://www.dnr.state.mn.us/education/geology/digging/agate.html" rel="noopener">Minnesota DNR, Lake Superior agate</a></p>
      </article>
      <article class="note">
        <span class="draft">Draft</span>
        <h2>Petoskey stone</h2>
        <p>A fossil colony of the coral <i>Hexagonaria percarinata</i> from a warm Devonian sea. Dry, it can pass for plain gray limestone. Wet, the six-sided cells, each with a dark center, jump out. It is Michigan's state stone.</p>
        <p>On the <a href="/products/great-lakes-rock-crew-tee">Great Lakes Rock Crew Tee</a> and the <a href="/products/pocket-full-of-treasures-tee">Pocket Full of Treasures Tee</a>.</p>
        <p class="src">Source: Michigan EGLE and Michigan Act 89 of 1965, per the project field guide draft.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="note">
        <span class="draft">Draft</span>
        <h2>Lake Superior agate</h2>
        <p>Banded chalcedony that filled gas bubbles in lava about 1.1 billion years ago, colored by iron. Dry, it is a dull, waxy, red-brown pebble. Hold a wet one up to the sun and a little light gets through. It is Minnesota's state gemstone.</p>
        <p class="src">Source: <a href="https://www.dnr.state.mn.us/education/geology/digging/agate.html" rel="noopener">Minnesota DNR, Lake Superior agate</a></p>
      </article>
      <article class="note">
        <span class="draft">Draft</span>
        <h2>Leland Blue</h2>
        <p>Not a natural stone at all. It is slag, glassy waste from an iron furnace that ran in Leland, Michigan, starting in 1870. Look for bubbles, glassy breaks, and blue, purple, and gray streaks. We love it anyway.</p>
        <p class="src">Source: project field guide draft, Leland Blue section.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="note">
        <span class="draft">Draft</span>
        <h2>Before it goes in your pocket</h2>
        <p>Rules depend on where you stand. Michigan allows up to 25 pounds of rocks and minerals per person per year from state-owned land, for personal use. Collecting is not allowed at Isle Royale, Apostle Islands, or Pictured Rocks. When in doubt, ask, or take a photo instead.</p>
        <p class="src">Sources: <a href="https://www.michigan.gov/egle/public/learn/geology/rockhounding" rel="noopener">Michigan EGLE, rockhounding</a>; National Park Service pages for each park (checked Nov 4, 2026).</p>
      </article>
